File: aruhan_logging_telemetry_engine.support
Classification: INTERNAL — TRUSTED ENGINEERS ONLY
Module: ARUHAN Logging & Telemetry Engine (Y‑Prime Edition)
1. Overview
The Logging & Telemetry Engine is ARUHAN’s system‑level observability layer, responsible for:

structured event logging

telemetry signal aggregation

module‑level performance tracking

drift and anomaly detection

system‑wide health monitoring

It operates beneath the System Orchestrator and receives signals from:

Emotional Engine

Memory Engine

Knowledge Engine

Task/Action Engine

Dialogue Engine

This engine does not influence behavior — it observes, records, and reports.

2. Logging Architecture
The engine maintains three independent logging channels:

event log — high‑level system events

telemetry log — numeric and meta‑state signals

anomaly log — drift, instability, or unexpected states

Each channel has:

fixed size limits

FIFO pruning

deterministic formatting

Logs are designed for internal debugging and future audit systems.

3. Event Logging
Event logs capture:

user messages

module outputs

orchestrator decisions

task transitions

emotional state changes

Each entry contains:

Code
timestamp  
event_type  
payload  
meta_snapshot  
Event logs are used for:

replaying system behavior

debugging module interactions

verifying pipeline order

4. Telemetry Logging
Telemetry logs capture numeric signals from all modules:

mood, stress, energy

memory drift, bias, coherence

knowledge graph size

task queue load

orchestrator sync score

Telemetry is stored as compact numeric snapshots for:

trend analysis

drift detection

performance monitoring

Telemetry logs are pruned aggressively to maintain efficiency.

5. Anomaly Detection
The engine monitors for anomalies such as:

excessive drift

low coherence

high load

rapid emotional swings

unstable task cycling

inconsistent module outputs

When detected, an anomaly entry is created:

Code
{"type": <string>, "value": <float>, "context": <dict>}
Anomalies do not trigger corrective action — they are recorded for higher‑level systems.

Future integration with the Stability & Recovery Engine will allow automated responses.

6. Telemetry Signals
The engine tracks the following signals:

emotional: mood, stress, energy

memory: drift, bias, coherence

knowledge: concept count, link count

task: queue size, active state

orchestrator: sync, load, bias

Signals are normalized to 0.0–1.0 where possible.

7. Rule Pipeline (Execution Order)
The Logging & Telemetry Engine follows this strict order:

receive module outputs

extract telemetry signals

write event log

write telemetry log

check for anomalies

write anomaly log

prune logs if needed

Changing the order will break consistency across logs.

8. Debugging Entry Points
Safe to inspect:

event log

telemetry log

anomaly log

signal summaries

Safe to modify:

log size limits

anomaly thresholds

telemetry sampling frequency

Do NOT modify:

log structure

anomaly detection rules

timestamping logic

pruning order

9. Safe Modification Zones
Engineers may safely:

add new telemetry fields

add new anomaly types

adjust thresholds

expand event categories

Engineers must NOT:

remove existing telemetry fields

disable anomaly detection

bypass timestamping

alter log formatting

10. Expansion Guidelines
When expanding logging capabilities:

maintain deterministic formatting

avoid storing raw user data unnecessarily

ensure compatibility with the System Orchestrator

test under high‑load scenarios

preserve FIFO pruning behavior

Future versions will integrate with:

external audit systems

recovery subsystems

long‑term analytics

11. Internal Philosophy
The Logging & Telemetry Engine is built on:

transparency

determinism

modular observability

minimal overhead

system‑wide clarity

It is not designed to influence ARUHAN — it is designed to observe ARUHAN.

12. Notes for Trusted Engineers
Logging is essential for debugging and long‑term evolution.

Never disable logging in production mode.

All modifications must be tested under stress conditions.

Future integration with the Stability Engine will add automated responses.
